#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/id #uuid "1ad81fed-5e71-5df6-abc4-a09cd5c432fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10362" :question/problem "Suppose that in a population of children with only one pet, $42\\%$ of children have a dog. A random sample of $n = 40$ children is taken from the population and asked what pet they have.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of children in the sample that have a dog? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/41/412ed703d6c44b1e664317fd734871cfe71d14c944cfa9f9bca84752f635836e.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}  = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p = 42 \\% = 0.42,$ and $n = 40.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =42%=0.42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{40} \\\\  & ≈0.0061\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161704 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/id #uuid "b162ec77-b73f-50f7-b2d2-b6c82bdb7d67" :question/math-academy-id "e-10363" :question/problem "Workers of a small company decided to elect a new union representative. There are $50$ workers, and $60\\%$ of this worker population will vote for candidate $A$. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the population will vote for candidateA; 0, otherwise$\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50$. Find $P(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0)$." :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N = 50$, and $p = 0.6$ represents the proportion of the population that will vote for candidate $A$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The number of members that will vote for candidate $A$ equals\n$Np = 50(0.6) = 30$.\n- The number of members that will *not* vote for candidate $A$ equals\n$N(1 - p) = 50(1 - 0.6) = 20$.\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0)\n$$\n\nthe probability that the second member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ given that the first member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ either.\n\nIf the first member does *not* vote for candidate $A$, we have\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nN - 1 = 50 - 1 = 49\n$$\n\nmembers remaining, and\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nN(1 - p)-1 = 19\n$$\n\nmembers remaining that will not vote for candidate $A$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0) = \\dfrac{N(1 - p)-1}{N-1} = \\dfrac{19}{49}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161705 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "a623014c-203f-5c40-87b5-e847782a8ea1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10364" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n = 360$ from a population where $35 \\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n![](images/9b/9b117bc5c640d338895493fde26f3c283d7752e1fcb7b0777a3e89a351808f6b.svg)\nwhere $i = 1, \\ldots, 360.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = \\sum_{i=1}^{360} X_i\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/ae/aee44a20433fe178eb22b1af7cf4d7e643b12bf4db69bd4b8a132cf8a59c50b3.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ the random variables $X_i$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p) \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X\\approx B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np = 35 \\% = 0.35, \\qquad n = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX\\approx B(360,0.35)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =360\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =126 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =360\\cdot 0.35\\cdot (1-0.35) \\\\  & =81.9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a748cda8-ea9d-5144-8d98-03dcd0282eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159753} {:answer/id #uuid "bc66e4d9-0c3b-5e1b-8243-547eba1e3923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159754} {:answer/id #uuid "33ecdc30-b74b-5b05-829b-acc4aa5bc7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159755} {:answer/id #uuid "617e3e5e-7c6d-5fec-b6c4-3a90708a4237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159756} {:answer/id #uuid "6eafe5c7-8902-5ff7-abe8-f0d1ba5d045a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a748cda8-ea9d-5144-8d98-03dcd0282eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159753} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0335b40-727a-5f83-9601-a606a9bb5b72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b52b200-3164-5ecb-b7e7-f226f8cd59f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-146167" :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N=20,$ where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,20.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=20,$ and $p=0.55$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=20(0.55)=11,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=20(1-0.55)=9.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1),$ the probability that the second member has the characteristic given that the first member also has the characteristic.\n\nIf the first member has the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=20-1=19$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np-1=11-1=10$ members remaining that have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1)=\\frac{Np-1}{N-1}=\\frac{10}{19}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0896db64-9d17-550f-8644-691706316afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441031} {:answer/id #uuid "174dc03d-1058-5222-a2c8-328de8d3dfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441036} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea9fa72-6c20-5814-8a9d-b2946f694c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441042} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee15f42-cfc1-5622-b98b-eb798bf29abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.55,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441049} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7e5957-681c-5abe-8f63-81da11f56105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.55,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "174dc03d-1058-5222-a2c8-328de8d3dfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441036} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff33034c-efba-5bcc-a6c7-948ce58e66bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fba7207c-8537-5b1a-b7be-0bdd1b79e13d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146295" :question/problem "Suppose that $45%$ of the voting population plans to vote for candidate $A$ in the upcoming mayoral election. A random sample of size $n=20$ is taken from the population and asked about their voting preference. \n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of voters in the sample that plan to vote for candidate $A?$ Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=45%=0.45,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =45%=0.45, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{20} \\\\  & ≈0.0124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "396108d7-e165-5e51-a218-82c7a7387856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=9,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=4.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441044} {:answer/id #uuid "75de330a-021b-5e46-ada2-d963d4b8b6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=11,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=4.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441060} {:answer/id #uuid "c9dc5707-7d2f-56a8-ab1f-d8df8930478d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=11,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=4.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441082} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f5f4b6-b070-593f-8481-3aeaeb2a2dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=11,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=3.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441083} {:answer/id #uuid "d17050cc-7332-556c-9082-471225f91b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=9,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=4.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75de330a-021b-5e46-ada2-d963d4b8b6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=11,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=4.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441060} :answer-field/id #uuid "38d82244-a4dd-54d0-9434-f6f13beddc2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe2c4a18-2ed9-51a4-8a01-2bc608b90a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-146303" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=20$ from a population where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,20.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=55%=0.55,\\,n=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(20,0.55)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.55\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =4.95\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "068df0c4-ece2-551e-830d-a33b8a7d322a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{74}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441030} {:answer/id #uuid "6a104a41-6eed-5c24-af7a-71a6fe434f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441058} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd97489-93fd-5274-88c5-b2b19f1359a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{74}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441062} {:answer/id #uuid "8503818a-9b86-5a35-aecb-0a2e8b2fe3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441066} {:answer/id #uuid "98d6ca49-3887-5fb0-bc6d-0177b078f0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8503818a-9b86-5a35-aecb-0a2e8b2fe3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441066} :answer-field/id #uuid "aefeda84-cb3e-54c7-a117-3708338c33ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a412ed0-e9f7-5318-a109-33611797297f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146348" :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N=75,$ where $80%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,75.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=75,$ and $p=0.8$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=75(0.8)=60,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=75(1-0.8)=15.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second member does not have the characteristic given that the first member does not have the characteristic either.\n\nIf the first member does not have the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=75-1=74$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $N(1-p)-1=14$ members remaining who do not have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{N(1-p)-1}{N-1}=\\frac{14}{74}=\\frac{7}{37}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd116ef-3346-54bc-b2fe-60c06789bd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441035} {:answer/id #uuid "1d71ebcd-0854-5420-800e-e81f47fa3a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{179}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441037} {:answer/id #uuid "7643c0f8-c498-5e7d-8da6-ee8fc999539f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441061} {:answer/id #uuid "df392b8d-2114-58ba-a19c-702b03b86bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{99}{179}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441089} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4eb93f-8444-5e43-b500-251824ded3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{98}{179}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d71ebcd-0854-5420-800e-e81f47fa3a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{179}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441037} :answer-field/id #uuid "89a7acda-4b0c-5959-9813-189a818f04e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dcf2c7ff-5863-50a2-8613-6f8998a6f4cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-146350" :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N=180,$ where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,180.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=180,$ and $p=0.45$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=180(0.45)=81,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=180(1-0.45)=99.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1),$ the probability that the second member has the characteristic given that the first member also has the characteristic.\n\nIf the first member has the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=180-1=179$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np-1=81-1=80$ members remaining that have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1)=\\frac{Np-1}{N-1}=\\frac{80}{179}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094ec17a-6309-5f98-acf9-535d8eb17412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441032} {:answer/id #uuid "62080c1c-fa0a-59e1-92cf-4e1e24714793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{69}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441055} {:answer/id #uuid "bf63837b-36e5-5303-a79a-c2032f377b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{69}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441079} {:answer/id #uuid "f47bceb2-4874-5deb-9f1d-7a538bff5d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441095} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6e404a-f691-5fa0-8933-b5686b52a70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f47bceb2-4874-5deb-9f1d-7a538bff5d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441095} :answer-field/id #uuid "e12b02a5-0ff9-56c2-b879-a7b367d95c2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6b56e16-5d64-5848-860d-3d44e0d9f335" :question/math-academy-id "q-146352" :question/problem "A box contains $70$ marbles, of which $30%$ are red and the rest are green. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th marble in the box is red} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,70.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=70,$ and $p=0.3$ represents the proportion of the red marbles.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of red marbles equals \n\n$$\nNp=70(0.3)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of green marbles equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=70(1-0.3)=49.\n$$\n\nWe select two marbles at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second marble is green given that the first marble is also green.\n\nIf the first marble is green, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=70-1=69$ marbles remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $N(1-p)-1=48$ marbles remaining that are green.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{N(1-p)-1}{N-1}=\\frac{48}{69}=\\frac{16}{23}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0608-9752-57c8-81ea-c146ec7ac93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441041} {:answer/id #uuid "530a30e5-6563-5fe0-b636-336bb2bc199c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441052} {:answer/id #uuid "82ad77ef-31e8-5b07-b94f-c8afee64c5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441064} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e62035-ace1-5772-89b1-118dc2b95cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441074} {:answer/id #uuid "f913f1e1-b98c-5a22-87cd-ede32839a4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f913f1e1-b98c-5a22-87cd-ede32839a4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441096} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b51b3f-c022-5825-8c4e-e77f4df70c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f68dd177-6fb9-5034-8aaa-03b4d052bc7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146378" :question/problem "A children's soccer academy has $60$ children enrolled. From this population, $35%$ are left-footed. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=60,$ and $p=0.35$ represents the proportion of the population of left-footed children.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of  left-footed children equals\n\n$$\nNp=60(0.35)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of right-footed children equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=60(1-0.35)=39.\n$$\n\nWe select two children of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second child is left-footed given that the first child is right-footed.\n\nIf the first child is right-footed, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=60-1=59$ children remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np=21$ children remaining who are left-footed.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{Np}{N-1}=\\frac{21}{59}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313d69b4-6852-5a13-b565-58db94680187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(130,0.35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441043} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5516fc-3456-5737-8aa9-330d31b2be53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(130,0.65)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441046} {:answer/id #uuid "754cb5ea-c2f6-5ee8-b725-93337b939d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(46,0.35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441059} {:answer/id #uuid "9c96fe6b-2a87-55d0-9ea0-ffcdcef3271e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441072} {:answer/id #uuid "d1480c9b-f23c-52a6-b9ab-57d01ff0f089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.65)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "313d69b4-6852-5a13-b565-58db94680187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(130,0.35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441043} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb58af87-7a8d-591a-a5c6-db3582a4b58b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a0ee3fc-f29f-5b1c-ac8e-c62ce346c425" :question/math-academy-id "q-146399" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=130$ from a population where $35%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,130.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{130}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=35%=0.35,\\,n=130.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(130,0.35).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a07f340-f0de-58a1-8eef-bde6af47d4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.48)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441045} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc1bec2-caea-5850-8341-0f2e65eb3ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(180,0.48)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441047} {:answer/id #uuid "604e4393-0850-5beb-aba7-64249646c2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(86,0.48)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441054} {:answer/id #uuid "c62e3614-4634-5ac6-ade8-e8b57ba9ba5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(180,0.52)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441081} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2f760c-4df1-5497-b091-4138bfc7801a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.52)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dc1bec2-caea-5850-8341-0f2e65eb3ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(180,0.48)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441047} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7223c07-8a22-51f9-b040-0c281e3688fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7cf9f35-9682-5170-9481-47202753d57f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146402" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=180$ from a population where $48%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,180.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{180}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=48%=0.48,\\,n=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(180,0.48).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6722c3cd-ec7f-5097-b55f-d96f54ca6df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=9,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=8.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441056} {:answer/id #uuid "99c04746-0912-59fe-8ac3-72bf6d45cc6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=10,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=7.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441071} {:answer/id #uuid "b3857716-e886-53a6-a471-de30e220e0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=10,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=8.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441077} {:answer/id #uuid "c25585ea-c83f-57ce-bd51-f45fd169466f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=9,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=7.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441080} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f6b528-5571-544d-9a05-17fb2cca69c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=9,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c25585ea-c83f-57ce-bd51-f45fd169466f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=9,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=7.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441080} :answer-field/id #uuid "c458ea31-a563-52a9-afec-c274ae8a1c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d151529b-f776-5a6f-8050-189f72b4d535" :question/math-academy-id "q-146404" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=45$ from a population where $20%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,45.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{45}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=20%=0.2,\\,n=45.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(45,0.2)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2\\cdot (1-0.2) \\\\  & =7.2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cda9d0b-c153-562d-ac1d-798cf69026f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=128,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=93.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441033} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6f11e6-66eb-560f-8f48-f16a3c3e83e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=128,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=87.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441048} {:answer/id #uuid "6846d9c1-083e-5354-8dbe-53b3e9a399b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=128,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=81.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441057} {:answer/id #uuid "7f65d500-2aeb-54b4-8dd1-a097b21dfc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=121,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=81.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441063} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a8b4bf-bfb7-56ab-86ea-c7c08d289e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=121,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=75.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e6f11e6-66eb-560f-8f48-f16a3c3e83e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=128,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=87.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441048} :answer-field/id #uuid "011c0d7f-8d89-5535-a87a-f90d68f3ba54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "669ea571-4e73-5129-bf0b-c10dd97639ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-146407" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=400$ from a population where $32%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,400.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{400}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=32%=0.32,\\,n=400.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(400,0.32)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =400\\cdot 0.32 \\\\  & =128 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =400\\cdot 0.32\\cdot (1-0.32) \\\\  & =87.04\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59127361-a7c6-54be-8be7-6e1bedd5c145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441053} {:answer/id #uuid "9073635c-c291-5d2a-89b8-35dca7c94744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441068} {:answer/id #uuid "afbdf9af-161b-565e-b86c-9d06f52deaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441075} {:answer/id #uuid "de1a4b59-e152-53b9-b98f-578e556fb269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441088} {:answer/id #uuid "e4313ddb-7ae8-58f9-81a8-402669642b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1a4b59-e152-53b9-b98f-578e556fb269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441088} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae3297d-2ab7-525a-83e9-568931f0c244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e62b0fb-05ae-5348-8fa8-26e1b9d8b974" :question/math-academy-id "q-146441" :question/problem "A bag contains a large number of equally-sized buttons, of which $15%$ are white. A random sample of $n=18$ buttons is taken from the bag.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of white buttons in the sample? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of buttons in the bag is significantly larger than the number in the sample.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=15%=0.15,$ and $n=18.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =15%=0.15, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{18} \\\\  & ≈0.007\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed2ddc3-b4dc-59ae-9d8b-0318dc6b3088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.78,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0069" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441038} {:answer/id #uuid "25672540-7f15-59d4-a9f0-a76441476fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.78,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441039} {:answer/id #uuid "4fecbd1a-4f33-567e-8978-268629ae9bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441051} {:answer/id #uuid "83135bc2-29fc-5570-8ca1-f6392afa94d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0096" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441065} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57b051-ab02-5e79-99b8-56f8c9fadb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0069" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec57b051-ab02-5e79-99b8-56f8c9fadb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0069" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441093} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e53a0ea-2177-529a-b607-5f54cb66e41c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "548372c4-2d00-514c-b6a7-43bd98885c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146447" :question/problem "Suppose that $22%$ of a particular population of teenagers like science fiction novels. A random sample of $n=25$ teenagers is taken from the population and asked about their favorite novel genres.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of teenagers in the sample who like science fiction novels? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=22%=0.22,$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =22%=0.22, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.22(1-0.22)}{25} \\\\  & ≈0.0069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c329a0-2b65-5553-80ed-a63f5cbd64ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441040} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfe0081-89a0-5f3a-8044-d6903a1aec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.9,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441067} {:answer/id #uuid "b32e31b8-7ee2-5888-8fe2-b6582e214786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441076} {:answer/id #uuid "e86497ec-cf36-5810-9fc4-eb1d50286589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.9,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441092} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbe8668-1cdb-5f50-82eb-12abca7abfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbe8668-1cdb-5f50-82eb-12abca7abfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441098} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14feaef-0c14-5d3c-a394-226d0415b9de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d736eeb-4230-5251-a2a5-38a3eb699594" :question/math-academy-id "q-146449" :question/problem "In a sports warehouse, a large container contains several thousand baseballs. According to a report from the manufacturer, $10%$ of these balls are defective. \n\nA random sample of size $n=20$ is taken from the container. What is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of defective balls in the sample?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of baseballs in the box is significantly larger than the number in the sample.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=10%=0.1,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =10%=0.1, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.1(1-0.1)}{20} \\\\  & =0.0045.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f582a26-8981-5aeb-8c9a-798c57cf7fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441034} {:answer/id #uuid "46e06021-ad9c-5a64-acca-08da3cae5944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.92,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441050} {:answer/id #uuid "94bc8e49-7700-5fe4-9f33-703b9393864d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441069} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c53a96-9533-5724-8502-b139f44c78a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.92,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441073} {:answer/id #uuid "d43f3441-cdd6-58be-bcdf-3e813693eab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f582a26-8981-5aeb-8c9a-798c57cf7fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441034} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48890ff-d63d-57c7-b77b-86bd5ac0b261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d5c4439-72cc-5a29-8805-27f9db8f5589" :question/math-academy-id "q-146450" :question/problem "Suppose that  $8%$ of a particular university's student population attends the law school. A random sample of $n=100$ students is taken from the population and asked which school they attend.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of students in the sample who attend the law school? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=8%=0.08,$ and $n=100.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =8%=0.08, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.08(1-0.08)}{100} \\\\  & ≈0.0007\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}]}
